    backlog:notYetScoreable true ; backlog:hasScoreabilityReason "Ranked by its story's state; scoring both would double-count." ;
    backlog:hasAuditNote "Specify the hybrid chains of a chapter as the gate Cross.B measures them, for the pilot chapter 6. Read the gate first: `08-tooling/sen0414_rdodi_gates_v1_1_1.py`, function gate_provenance (Cross.B): what it calls a hybrid chain (a chain that crosses the research document, the domain ontology and the page ABox), which links it follows, what it counts as checked and as broken, and why it prints CANNOT_RUN with 0 chains. Then write the specification as SHACL and data, not as markdown (rulings are never kept as markdown): a NEW file `08-tooling/crossb/crossb_chain_shapes_v1_0_0.ttl` (SHACL shapes of a hybrid chain exactly as the gate reads it) and a NEW file `08-tooling/crossb/crossb_chain_model_v1_0_0.json` (for chapter 6: the proposed chains as ordered lists of the existing individuals of its research files and its page ABox 03-materials/ch06/page/sen0414_ch06_page_abox_v1_4_0.ttl, each chain with the links that would be added, and the count of chains). A chain uses only links to existing individuals (links are links, never text); nothing is invented; no gate, test or threshold is edited; if the gate cannot count any chain without an edit of the gate, STOP and report with the line numbers. Report what the gate would print for the proposed chains by running the gate on a scratch copy of the graph (no committed file is touched). LESSONS OF ITERATION 19 (the rules of this iteration): (1) A committed file is never edited - and that includes your OWN tool scripts, builders, data modules, test drivers and logs: every new version of any of them is a NEW file with a new version number FROM THE FIRST SAVE (do not save vX, commit it and then edit it; the controller found two such in-place edits by git and they were disclosed in the register); only a file you have not yet committed may be regenerated. (2) Report negative results first and as printed; a skip is not a pass; Cross.B CANNOT_RUN is not a pass. (3) The machine has two cores shared by up to three executors: every browser test runs under `flock /tmp/page_test.lock`; no build or corpus run beside a browser test of yours; a timing-sensitive check can fail under load - say so, rerun on a quiet machine, keep the failing log. (4) Everything you cite in your report is a commit you made and a file that exists; the controller re-runs every claim, so a report that says more than the files show is NOT MET. Planned session SEN0414-I20-crossb-spec. Estimated 3.0 hours (an estimate, G120: nobody has witnessed a start; the real start is recorded at work begun). Takes a place only when no higher-ranked task can start." .

