ex:TK_Ret_Ch10_Deck_Build backlog:hasAuditNote "Controlled by the lineage agent on 2026-10-25T10:27:36 UTC: the chapter 10 deck (03-materials/ch10/SEN0414_Ch10_ReadingWritingFiles_3e_v2_0_0.pptx, 41 slides, 18 examples, 7 native charts, 4 stories, with the chapter-code manifest 03-materials/ch10/page/chapter_code_ch10_v1_0_0.json; commit 2de39cd of the executor session SEN0414-I20-deck-ch10; the earlier deck 08-tooling/ch10-deck/sen0414_ch10_deck_v1_0_0.pptx stays as committed): the controller's own re-runs - the deck standard check v1_0_0 prints SUMMARY PASS=13 FAIL=0 NOT_MEASURED=0 including M1 (the manifest equals the deck's code boxes, text and order, 18 of 18; the earlier deck failed 7 elements), the overlap check v1_0_0 ends CLEAN (0 findings; the earlier deck had 2), the first-line error scan of the manifest ends PASS (0 of 18 examples whose recorded result begins with an error line), the 18 examples run now under Python 3.13.16 in a fresh interpreter print exactly the recorded result, the 4 story pictures (photographs and the Python logo) cover the 4 story slides with a provenance record, no committed file of the chapter is modified since the register commit that planned Iteration 20 (14 files added), everything is committed; the controller rendered all 41 slides to images (LibreOffice) and read a selection (title, analogy tables, code with the executed result as drawn diagram and native chart, a story slide with a photograph and the 5N1K boxes, the practice program, the resources slide): they are legible, numbered, with explanation beside each listing. THE EXECUTOR'S QUESTION (the shelve example): shelve.open cannot run in the page's Pyodide (no dbm module), so the example uses shelve.Shelf over a plain dict; the controller judges it acceptable against the book's chapter - the slide says in its own text and its footer that shelve.open('mydata') puts the same dictionary in a file and that a plain dict plays the file here, so no claim is made that is not true, the traps of the book (a list changed in place is not saved) are executed, and the real call is named; a live demonstration of the file itself is an enhancement and not required by the criterion. Observations, not failures: the Python logo stands for the Pitrou/PEP 428 story (no free photo; the licence is recorded); the story slide 31 says 'written up in 2001' and gives 12 November 2002 for json.org, which reads as two dates - the facts are right (the first JSON message in 2001, json.org in 2002) and the title could say it. The executor did not run the page test, the story probe or Research/Ch10: they belong to the page and research tasks.. Evidence ex:Ev2_Ctl_deck10, verified by backlog_evidence_bridge_v1_1_0." .
